# Custom Notification Stack — Implementation Plan

## 0. Decision: sonner stays installed, but fully unmounted from the app

Remove the `<Toaster>` mount and the `sonner` import from `src/App.tsx`. Do **not**
touch `src/components/ui/sonner.tsx` (CLAUDE.md forbids editing generated `ui/*`
files) and do **not** run `npm uninstall sonner` — leave the dependency in
`package.json` untouched. Rationale: `ui/sonner.tsx` is shadcn-generated and
off-limits either way; removing the npm dependency is a separate, riskier,
unnecessary step (could affect lockfile / other future shadcn regen) for zero
runtime benefit once nothing imports it. Net effect: sonner code is simply dead
weight in `node_modules`, nothing imports `sonner` or `ui/sonner.tsx` anymore.

## 1. New files

- `src/utils/notificationStore.ts` — plain module-level store (no state lib),
  mirrors the pattern already used for cross-component communication in this
  repo (module state + subscriber list, same spirit as the `window.dispatchEvent`
  convention, but kept as a direct import since `toast.ts` and
  `NotificationStack.tsx` are tightly coupled 1:1 — no need to round-trip through
  `window` events for this internal wiring; `window` events stay reserved for the
  existing cross-component signal bus per CLAUDE.md convention).
- `src/components/NotificationStack.tsx` — the stack UI, mounted once in
  `App.tsx` in place of `<Toaster>`.
- `src/components/NotificationStack.module.scss` — glass styling, following the
  `.bar`/`.toolbar` recipe.

`src/utils/toast.ts` is edited (not replaced) to call into the new store instead
of `sonner`, keeping exact existing exported signatures.

## 2. Store shape (`src/utils/notificationStore.ts`)

```ts
export type NotificationVariant = 'success' | 'error' | 'warning' | 'loading';

export interface NotificationItem {
  id: string;
  variant: NotificationVariant;
  message: string;
  createdAt: number; // Date.now(), used only for stable ordering
}

type Listener = (items: NotificationItem[]) => void;

let items: NotificationItem[] = [];
// Index into `items` (by id) of the notification currently pinned to front by
// the wheel gesture. `null` = no manual override, front = most recent (last
// pushed) — see §5 for how this interacts with new arrivals.
let frontId: string | null = null;
const listeners = new Set<Listener>();

function emit() {
  const snapshot = items.slice();
  listeners.forEach((l) => l(snapshot));
}

let uid = 0;
function genId() {
  uid += 1;
  return `ntf-${Date.now()}-${uid}`;
}

export function pushNotification(variant: NotificationVariant, message: string): string {
  const id = genId();
  items = [...items, { id, variant, message, createdAt: Date.now() }];
  frontId = id; // a new notification always becomes the front card
  emit();
  return id;
}

/** Used by showLoading's returned id + dismissToast, and by the × button. */
export function removeNotification(id: string): void {
  const idx = items.findIndex((n) => n.id === id);
  if (idx === -1) return;
  items = items.filter((n) => n.id !== id);
  if (frontId === id) frontId = null; // stack re-derives front (see selector)
  emit();
}

/** Called by NotificationStack's wheel handler. */
export function setFrontNotification(id: string): void {
  if (!items.some((n) => n.id === id)) return;
  frontId = id;
  emit();
}

export function subscribe(listener: Listener): () => void {
  listeners.add(listener);
  listener(items.slice());
  return () => listeners.delete(listener);
}

export function getFrontId(): string | null {
  return frontId;
}
```

Notes:
- `loading` variant is kept only because `showLoading`/`dismissToast` must keep
  working (grep shows these two are currently unused at call sites, but they are
  part of the public `toast.ts` API and must not regress). It renders like the
  others but conventionally callers immediately follow up with
  `dismissToast(id)` once work finishes — no spinner infra needed beyond a
  small rotating icon in the card if desired; not a requirement, keep it simple
  (static "loading" styling, e.g. a subtle pulsing dot) since these permanent
  cards have no timer to race against.
- Ordering is push order (`items` array, oldest first, newest last). The stack
  always visually derives "who's on top" from `frontId ?? items[items.length-1]?.id`
  (see §4), so a fresh `pushNotification` always naturally becomes front without
  extra logic in the component.

## 3. `src/utils/toast.ts` (edited, same exported signatures)

```ts
import { pushNotification, removeNotification } from './notificationStore';

export const showSuccess = (message: string) => {
  pushNotification('success', message);
};

export const showError = (message: string) => {
  pushNotification('error', message);
};

export const showWarning = (message: string) => {
  pushNotification('warning', message);
};

export const showLoading = (message: string) => {
  return pushNotification('loading', message);
};

export const dismissToast = (toastId: string) => {
  removeNotification(toastId);
};
```

All ~25 existing call sites (AxisSidebar.tsx, use-gcode-preview.ts,
CategoriesSidebar.tsx, Index.tsx, ModelingSidebar.tsx, PrinterConfigSidebar.tsx,
TextureEditorModal.tsx, Viewport3D.tsx, ViewCube.tsx) need **zero** changes —
they only import `showSuccess`/`showError`/`showWarning`/`showLoading`/
`dismissToast` from `@/utils/toast`, whose signatures are unchanged.

## 4. `src/components/NotificationStack.tsx`

### Responsibilities
- Subscribe to `notificationStore` for the item list.
- Track `frontId` (read via a second small subscription or derive locally by
  re-reading `getFrontId()` on each store emit — simplest: extend the store's
  `subscribe` payload to also pass `frontId`, i.e. `Listener = (items, frontId) => void`;
  update the store code in §2 accordingly. This avoids a second store/race.)
- Render the stack container, anchored bottom-right identically to the old
  sonner mount: same `right: 24px`, `bottom: 84px` offset logic as documented in
  `App.tsx`'s comment, reimplemented as fixed positioning in the SCSS module
  (`position: fixed; right: 24px; bottom: 84px;`).
- Compute per-card stack position (`stackIndex`, 0 = front) and render only a
  capped number of "peek" cards behind the front one (see below).
- Attach a `onWheel` handler on the stack container.
- Render nothing (`return null`) when `items.length === 0`.

### State shape inside the component

```tsx
const [items, setItems] = useState<NotificationItem[]>([]);
const [frontId, setFrontIdState] = useState<string | null>(null);

useEffect(() => subscribeToNotifications((its, front) => {
  setItems(its);
  setFrontIdState(front);
}), []);
```

### Deriving render order (front → back)

```ts
const MAX_PEEK = 3; // front + up to 3 peeking behind = 4 cards max rendered

const orderedIds = useMemo(() => {
  if (items.length === 0) return [];
  const ids = items.map((n) => n.id);
  const resolvedFront = frontId && ids.includes(frontId) ? frontId : ids[ids.length - 1];
  // Cycle order starting at resolvedFront, going toward older items, wrapping
  // to the newest-first remainder — i.e. a ring buffer read starting at front.
  const frontIdx = ids.indexOf(resolvedFront);
  const before = ids.slice(0, frontIdx).reverse(); // older-than-front, nearest first... 
  const after = ids.slice(frontIdx + 1).reverse();  // newer-than-front, nearest first
  // "Nearest in stack order" = whichever is adjacent by push-order distance;
  // simplest well-defined rule: everything else ordered by recency (excluding
  // front), newest-first. This makes wheel cycling predictable (see §5).
  const rest = [...ids].filter((id) => id !== resolvedFront).reverse();
  return [resolvedFront, ...rest];
}, [items, frontId]);

const visibleIds = orderedIds.slice(0, MAX_PEEK + 1); // front + 3 peeking
```

Each id in `visibleIds` gets a `stackIndex` = its position in that array (0 =
front, fully drawn/interactive; 1..3 = peeking slivers, `pointer-events: none`
except the close × — see §7 for whether peeking cards' × is reachable: **decision:
peeking cards are not interactive at all, only the front card has a live × and
receives wheel; to close a specific non-front notification the user must first
scroll it to front**. This keeps hit-testing simple and matches "cards are
stacked, wheel brings one to front" mental model; revisit only if user wants
direct-click-to-close on peeking cards later).

Cards beyond `MAX_PEEK+1` (if the stack grows large) are not rendered at all —
purely so DOM stays small; they still exist in `items` and become visible again
as they cycle within the top 4 via wheel (see §5, cycling reads from full
`orderedIds`, only rendering is capped).

### Per-card transform (the "peek" visual)

For `stackIndex` `i` in `0..3`:

```ts
const PEEK_OFFSET_X = 10; // px, rightward creep per layer back (edges peek from behind-left is wrong: reference shows top/right edges peeking, so offset is up+right per the drawing: negative Y, positive X)
const PEEK_OFFSET_Y = -10; // px per layer, cards behind rise slightly up
const SCALE_STEP = 0.05;
const OPACITY_STEP = 0.18;

style = {
  zIndex: 100 - i,
  transform: `translate(${i * PEEK_OFFSET_X}px, ${i * PEEK_OFFSET_Y}px) scale(${1 - i * SCALE_STEP})`,
  opacity: Math.max(0.15, 1 - i * OPACITY_STEP),
}
```

This matches the user's reference drawing: rectangles fanned so the top-right
edge of each card-behind peeks out past the front card's top-right corner.
(Card 0 = full size/opacity/front; cards 1-3 progressively smaller, more
transparent, offset up-and-right, and covered by everything in front of them
because they're positioned earlier — i.e. visually *behind* — in paint order:
lower `zIndex`.)

Animate all three properties (`transform`, `opacity`) with `framer-motion`
(`motion.div`, already a project dependency — used elsewhere for space
transitions per `use-gcode-preview.ts`/`Sidebar.tsx` patterns) rather than raw
CSS transitions, because:
- Cards need `layout` animation when the stack reflows (an item removed from
  the middle, or wheel changes front) — `motion.div layout` handles this
  automatically across reordering, which plain CSS transitions on
  React-key-based re-renders would fight against (React would need to keep DOM
  node identity stable across reorders, `AnimatePresence`+`layout` is exactly
  built for this).
- Use `<AnimatePresence mode="popLayout">` wrapping the mapped cards, `motion.div`
  per card keyed by `item.id`, with `initial`/`animate`/`exit` variants:
  - `initial`: `{ opacity: 0, y: 20, scale: 0.9 }` (new card slides up from
    below the stack, consistent with "arrives from outside")
  - `animate`: the computed `{ x, y, scale, opacity }` for its current
    `stackIndex` (framer interpolates automatically on index change — this is
    what produces the fluid wheel-driven slide, see §5)
  - `exit`: `{ opacity: 0, x: 40, scale: 0.85, transition: { duration: 0.18 } }`
    (close × slides card out to the right and fades)
  - `transition`: `{ type: 'spring', stiffness: 380, damping: 34 }` for the
    stack-position changes (front-swap on wheel), for a snappy-but-fluid feel.

### JSX sketch

```tsx
<div className={styles.stack} onWheel={handleWheel}>
  <AnimatePresence mode="popLayout">
    {visibleIds.map((id, stackIndex) => {
      const item = items.find((n) => n.id === id)!;
      return (
        <motion.div
          key={id}
          className={styles.card}
          data-variant={item.variant}
          style={{ zIndex: 100 - stackIndex }}
          initial={{ opacity: 0, y: 20, scale: 0.9 }}
          animate={{
            x: stackIndex * PEEK_OFFSET_X,
            y: stackIndex * PEEK_OFFSET_Y,
            scale: 1 - stackIndex * SCALE_STEP,
            opacity: Math.max(0.15, 1 - stackIndex * OPACITY_STEP),
          }}
          exit={{ opacity: 0, x: 40, scale: 0.85 }}
          transition={{ type: 'spring', stiffness: 380, damping: 34 }}
        >
          {stackIndex === 0 && (
            <>
              <NotificationIcon variant={item.variant} />
              <span className={styles.message}>{item.message}</span>
              <button
                className={styles.close}
                aria-label="Fermer"
                onClick={() => removeNotification(id)}
              >
                <X size={14} />
              </button>
            </>
          )}
          {stackIndex > 0 && <div className={styles.peekFill} />}
        </motion.div>
      );
    })}
  </AnimatePresence>
</div>
```

(`peekFill` = an empty opaque glass rectangle for non-front cards — no need to
render icon/text/close for cards the user can't read anyway, since they're
mostly hidden behind the front card and scaled/faded; this also sidesteps any
accessibility noise from off-screen-ish duplicate text.)

## 5. Wheel-cycling logic (`handleWheel`)

Interpretation chosen (documented explicitly per the task's request for a
concrete interpretation): **scrolling down (`deltaY > 0`) cycles to the next
card "further back" in the current visual stack (i.e., the current `stackIndex
1` card becomes front); scrolling up (`deltaY < 0`) brings back the
previously-front card / cycles the other direction.** This matches the natural
"scroll down = go forward/deeper into the stack" convention used elsewhere
(e.g. carousels), and reverses cleanly on scroll-up so users aren't stuck
cycling one direction only.

```ts
const handleWheel = (e: React.WheelEvent) => {
  if (orderedIds.length < 2) return;
  e.preventDefault(); // stack is a fixed-position overlay, never let this scroll page content behind it
  const frontIdx = 0; // orderedIds[0] is always current front by construction
  const delta = e.deltaY > 0 ? 1 : -1;
  const nextIdx = ((frontIdx + delta) % orderedIds.length + orderedIds.length) % orderedIds.length;
  setFrontNotification(orderedIds[nextIdx]);
};
```

Because `orderedIds` is recomputed from `frontId` + recency each render (§4),
calling `setFrontNotification(nextId)` triggers a store emit → component
re-render → `orderedIds` reshuffles so `nextId` is now at index 0 → every
card's `stackIndex` (and therefore its `animate` target `x/y/scale/opacity`)
changes → framer-motion's spring transition animates each card smoothly from
its old slot to its new slot. This is what produces "la molette les fait
glisser dans une animation fluide" — no manual animation code needed beyond the
`animate` prop already being data-driven by `stackIndex`.

Debounce: wheel events fire rapidly during a single physical scroll gesture.
Add a small guard so one "tick" of a mouse wheel (or one trackpad flick) only
advances the front by one position, not several:

```ts
const wheelLockRef = useRef(false);
const handleWheel = (e: React.WheelEvent) => {
  if (orderedIds.length < 2) return;
  e.preventDefault();
  if (wheelLockRef.current) return;
  wheelLockRef.current = true;
  setTimeout(() => { wheelLockRef.current = false; }, 220); // matches spring settle time roughly
  const delta = e.deltaY > 0 ? 1 : -1;
  const nextIdx = ((0 + delta) % orderedIds.length + orderedIds.length) % orderedIds.length;
  setFrontNotification(orderedIds[nextIdx]);
};
```

### Interaction with new arrivals while mid-stack item is focused to front

Per §2/§3, `pushNotification` unconditionally sets `frontId = <new id>`. This
means: **a new notification always interrupts and takes over the front slot**,
even if the user had manually scrolled an older one to front. This is the
simplest, most predictable rule (new events are, by definition, the most
important/recent thing happening) and avoids the ambiguity of "should a new
arrival respect the user's manual pin". It also matches how the old sonner
stack behaved (new toast always appears at the visually top position). The
previously-front (user-pinned) card animates back into the peek stack exactly
like any other reordering, via the same `stackIndex` recompute + framer spring.

## 6. Close (×) button behavior

`onClick={() => removeNotification(id)}` on the front card only (§4 decision).
`removeNotification`:
- Splices the item out of `items`.
- If the removed id was `frontId`, resets `frontId = null` so the selector in
  §4 (`resolvedFront = frontId && ids.includes(frontId) ? frontId : ids[ids.length-1]`)
  falls back to "most recent remaining item" as the new front — a sensible
  default (front card closes → next-most-recent becomes front) without needing
  extra "what should become front next" bookkeeping.
- Triggers `AnimatePresence`'s `exit` animation (slide right + fade) for the
  removed card while every remaining card's `stackIndex` shifts down by one and
  re-animates into its new slot via the same spring — a natural "stack reflow".

## 7. Blur-without-ghosting — resolution of the open question

**Conclusion: no special stacking-context/isolation trickery is required.**
`backdrop-filter` only samples pixels already painted behind the element within
the same compositing context, at the moment of paint. Because:
- Every card (front and peeking) in this design is rendered with a fully
  **opaque-enough background** (`rgba(10, 15, 26, 0.25)` — this is NOT fully
  opaque, it's translucent by design, matching the existing glass recipe), and
- Cards behind the front one are painted **first** (lower `zIndex`, earlier in
  paint order) and are positioned so the front card fully covers their
  bounding box except for the small peek slivers,

...the front card's `backdrop-filter: blur(4px)` will correctly sample: (a) the
3D viewport wherever no card is behind it, and (b) the peeking cards'
own semi-translucent glass fill wherever they *do* stick out from behind the
front card. That second case is not "ghosting" — it's the intended visual (the
peek slivers are supposed to be visible; they're a deliberate part of the
stacked-deck look, not a leak to avoid). The actual risk the task is flagging —
peeking cards' TEXT/icons showing blurred through the front card — is
structurally impossible in this design because **only the front card renders
text/icon content** (§4, `stackIndex === 0 &&` guard); peeking cards render an
empty `.peekFill` div, so there is no content behind the front card that could
"ghost" through the blur except the flat glass-tinted rectangles, which is the
desired peek effect itself.

The one real requirement to call out for the SCSS: give `.stack` (the
container) `isolation: isolate;` — not to fix ghosting (there is none, by the
above), but purely defensive/for-free consistency with the existing codebase
convention (`GCodePreviewBar.module.scss`'s `.bar` also sets `isolation:
isolate;`), ensuring the stack's own z-index layering never interacts
unpredictably with sibling fixed-position chrome (SliceSidebar toolbar,
GCodePreviewBar) that might also create stacking contexts.

## 8. SCSS (`src/components/NotificationStack.module.scss`)

Follow the exact glass recipe from `GCodePreviewBar.module.scss`/
`SliceSidebar.module.scss`:

```scss
.stack {
  position: fixed;
  right: 24px;
  bottom: 84px;
  z-index: 60; // above viewport chrome, consistent with other floating panels
  isolation: isolate;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  align-items: flex-end;
  pointer-events: none; // re-enabled per-card below so empty space isn't a hit target
}

.card {
  position: absolute; // stacked via transform, not flex flow — flow would fight framer-motion's layout animation
  right: 0;
  bottom: 0;
  min-width: 280px;
  max-width: 360px;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 10px;
  padding: 12px 14px;
  border-radius: 14px;
  border: none;
  box-shadow: 0 8px 32px 0 rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.4), 0 2px 8px 0 rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3);
  background: rgba(10, 15, 26, 0.25);
  backdrop-filter: blur(4px);
  -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(4px);
  color: rgb(212, 212, 216);
  pointer-events: auto;

  &:not(:first-child) {
    pointer-events: none; // peeking cards are inert, see §4/§5
  }
}

.peekFill {
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  border-radius: inherit;
}

.message {
  flex: 1;
  font-size: 13px;
  line-height: 1.4;
}

.close {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  width: 22px;
  height: 22px;
  border-radius: 6px;
  background: transparent;
  border: none;
  color: rgba(212, 212, 216, 0.7);
  cursor: pointer;
  &:hover { background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08); color: rgb(212, 212, 216); }
}

// Variant accent (left icon color only — keep the glass bg identical across
// variants per "matching the rest of the chrome", don't reintroduce sonner's
// colored-background style):
.card[data-variant='success'] svg { color: #4ade80; }
.card[data-variant='error'] svg { color: #f87171; }
.card[data-variant='warning'] svg { color: #fbbf24; }
.card[data-variant='loading'] svg { color: #a1a1aa; }
```

Since `.card` uses `position: absolute` with the transform-driven offsets from
§4 (not flex-gap stacking), stack container `.stack` needs a fixed `height`/`width`
reservation equal to one card's footprint (e.g. `width: 360px; height: 64px;`)
so `position: fixed` on `.stack` anchors correctly regardless of card count —
or alternatively skip a fixed size and let `.card`'s own `position: absolute;
right:0; bottom:0;` inside a zero-size `.stack` anchor correctly (simpler,
recommended: `.stack { position: fixed; right: 24px; bottom: 84px; width: 0;
height: 0; }`, each `.card` anchored via `right: 0; bottom: 0;` relative to
that 0×0 point, growing left/up as needed via `min-width`/`max-width`).

